  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 252 PM CDT Fri Oct 23 2026\n\n     TONIGHT THROUGH MONDAY NIGHT...\n\nA fairly amplified pattern remains over the Lower 48 this afternoon \nwith deep troughing over the western CONUS and strong mid/upper \nridging over the central CONUS. Increased meridional flow over the \nRockies is promoting gradual pressure falls across the Front Range \nand High Plains leading to an increased pressure gradient across the \ncentral plains. As a result, southerly low level flow has increased, \nand many are experiencing a breezy day today with sustained winds \naround 10 to 20 mph with gusts around 30 to 35 mph at times, \nespecially across central Kansas. While winds will decrease tonight, \nlow-level flow will remain southerly, and lows will only dip into \nthe mid 50s to low 60s as a result. A weak frontal boundary will sag \ninto Kansas on Saturday; however, much of the moisture and broad \nascent associated with the incoming trough will be out over eastern \nColorado and western Kansas. While, a stray shower cannot be ruled \nout as far east as US-281, most of the activity should remain well \nto the west throughout the day. That being said, increased cloud \ncover should keep afternoon highs in the 70s on Saturday.\n\nShort range guidance indicates a couple of embedded vort maxes will \nround the based of the aforementioned mid/upper trough and move out \nover the High Plains both Sunday and Monday. This increased synoptic \nascent along with increasing amounts of moisture being pulled \nnorthward from the eastern Pacific will support widespread showers \nacross western, central, and portions of south-central Kansas Sunday \nand Monday. The heaviest rainfall will primarily be across western \nKansas, but rainfall totals up to 0.25\" are still possible across \nportions of central Kansas. Farther south and east, coverage of \nshowers is forecast to drop off quite a bit, and rainfall totals \nacross the Flint Hills and south-central Kansas will likely remain \nbelow 0.10\" through Monday. Meanwhile, a cold front will slowly sag \nacross the region bringing a slightly cooler airmass to the area. \nWhile highs on Sunday will remain in the mid to upper 70s, highs on \nMonday will be slightly cooler with highs in the mid 60s to low 70s.\n\n     TUESDAY THROUGH NEXT WEEKEND...\n\nMid to long range guidance continues to show some marginal \nopportunities at isolated to scattered showers through the remainder \nof next week. A couple of weak frontal passages may be enough to \ntrigger some showers early Wednesday, and again late Thursday or \nearly Friday. Regardless, coverage of shower activity will be \nlimited, and meaningful rainfall appears unlikely. Temperatures will \ncontinue to remain near average through the middle to latter \nportions of next week. Long range guidance continues to signal the \npotential for a rather vigorous system moving into the southern and \ncentral plains Halloween Weekend. It's still way too early to get \ninto the finer details, but at this moment, there's an increased \nlikelihood of widespread rainfall heading into next weekend.",
